<main class="huge">
  <header>
    <h1 class="main-heading large">
      Liquify your
      <span class="huge">life</span>
    </h1>
  </header>

  <article>
    {% for manager in managers %}
      {{ manager }}
    {% endfor %}

    {% comment %} will add TOC later {% endcomment %}
    {% comment %} <h2>Table of contents</h2> {% endcomment %}

    <h2>Installation</h2>
    <p>Firstly create a directory and enter it with the following commands:</p>
    {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'mkdir my-static-site', type: 'console' %}
    {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'cd my-static-site', type: 'console' %}

    {% render 'molecule/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_init', cmd: 'init', managers: managers %}

    <p>Then open up your terminal and get the following packages ready:</p>

    {% render 'molecule/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_liquidjs', cmd: 'add liquidjs', managers: managers %}
    {% render 'molecule/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_11ty', cmd: 'add -D @11ty/eleventy', managers: managers %}

    <h2>Configuring Eleventy (v3+)</h2>
    <p>
      Modern Eleventy relies on ES Modules (ESM) by default. Create an explicit configuration file named
      {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'eleventy.config.js' %} in your project root and add the modern
      {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'export default' %} structure:
    </p>

    <pre><code>export default function (eleventyConfig) {
  return {
    dir: {
      input: "src",
      output: "_site"
    }
  };
};</code></pre>

    <p>
      This explicitly tells Eleventy to process everything inside your
      {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'src' %} directory and compile the optimized, production-ready static
      assets directly into {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: '_site' %}.
    </p>

    <h2>Your First Template</h2>
    <p>Create your source folder structure and your primary template file:</p>
    {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'mkdir src', type: 'console' %}
    {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'touch src/index.liquid', type: 'console' %}

    <p>
      Open up {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'src/index.liquid' %} and add a front matter block along with a
      dynamic Liquid array loop to test parsing capabilities:
    </p>
    <pre><code>&lt;---
title: Welcome to Liquify
---&gt;
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;&#123;&#123; title &#125;&#125;&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;&#123;&#123; title &#125;&#125;&lt;/h1&gt;
  
  &#123;% assign array = "HTML, CSS, Liquid" | split: ", " %&#125;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &#123;% for item in array %&#125;
      &lt;li&gt;Rendering: &#123;&#123; item &#125;&#125;&lt;/li&gt;
    &#123;% endfor %&#125;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>

    <h2>Fire Up the Build Server</h2>
    <p>
      To keep development moving quickly, add a start script execution path directly into your
      {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'package.json' %} file:
    </p>
    <pre><code>"scripts": {
  "start": "eleventy --serve"
}</code></pre>

    <p>
      Now, run the script from your terminal using your active workflow package manager to boot up the hot-reloading
      development server:
    </p>

    <section data-swapping>
      {% render 'molecule/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_run', cmd: 'start', managers: managers %}
    </section>

    <p>
      The Eleventy compiler will now build your site and launch a hot-reloading dashboard locally at
      {% render 'atom/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'http://localhost:8080', type: 'console' %}. Open it up and start
      hacking!
    </p>
  </article>
</main>
